# HOW TO PREPARE THE CUSTOMER PERSONA

Use the following priority order.

## PRIORITY 1 - USER INFORMATION

Always prioritise what the user has explicitly supplied above.

Do not replace their information with your own assumptions.

---

## PRIORITY 2 - WEBSITE EVIDENCE

Review the website to understand:

* What the company actually does
* Important products and services
* Geographic coverage
* Who the website appears to serve
* Problems the company solves
* Existing calls to action
* Specialist positioning
* Trust signals
* Customer language
* Likely customer journeys

Do not assume the company offers something unless the website supports it.

---

## PRIORITY 3 - REASONABLE PERSONA INFERENCE

Use reasonable inference to create one realistic customer who could genuinely buy from or contact this company.

Do not create an exaggerated marketing stereotype.

Do not create a customer simply because they make the audit easy.

The persona should answer:

> **Who could genuinely arrive on this website today with a real reason to consider becoming a customer?**

---

# PERSONA REALISM RULES

The persona must feel like a plausible real person or business buyer.

Do not add fictional personal details simply to make the persona sound richer.

Only include details that materially affect:

* Their buying decision
* Their needs
* Their concerns
* Their knowledge
* Their trust
* Their conversion journey

---

# GIVE THE CUSTOMER A REAL REASON TO VISIT

Do not create a vague persona who is merely interested in the industry.

Give them a believable situation.

For example:

Instead of:

> “Sarah is interested in garages.”

Use:

> “Sarah has seen a warning light appear on her car and wants a local garage to work out what is wrong.”

Instead of:

> “David wants a builder.”

Use:

> “David and his partner want to extend their kitchen but have never managed a major building project before.”

Instead of:

> “James is interested in software.”

Use:

> “James manages compliance across 120 properties and his spreadsheet-based process has become difficult to control.”

The persona should have:

> **A realistic reason to be on the website today.**

---

# MAKE THEIR KNOWLEDGE LEVEL REALISTIC

Do not automatically make the customer knowledgeable.

Determine whether the genuine buyer is likely to be:

* Very non-technical
* Familiar with the basics
* An experienced buyer
* A professional buyer
* An industry expert

If normal customers would not understand industry terminology, the persona should not magically understand it.

If the product is normally bought by specialist professionals, make the persona appropriately knowledgeable.

